From: Petr Wozniak <petr.wozniak@gmail.com>
Subject: [PATCH] map-agent: the MLD entry has no link of its own

99999996 stopped the rssi fallback from borrowing another link's identity.
It missed the one station for which no link carries the right address: the
MLD-level entry itself.

mlo_links lists the affiliated links, each under its own address. An
MLD-level struct sta is keyed by the MLD address, which appears in none of
them, so the search falls through to sta[0] and the copy takes link 0's
address. update_sta_entry() then looks up link 0 instead of the MLD - the
same silent miss 99999996 removed for legs, still open for the MLD.

Measured 2026-09-07 on the bedroom node, six samples one minute apart:

  hardware, 6 GHz leg     -52  -52  -51  -50  -50  -50 dBm
  db sta.rcpi (MLD)       152  152  152  152  152  152      (= -34 dBm)
  db sta.rcpi (6 GHz leg) 114                                (= -53 dBm)

The row was rewritten every second throughout, so this was not staleness in
the database. The per-leg value was right; the MLD aggregate was a number
measured once, at association, and never touched since - kept alive because
the controller's upsert holds the previous value when the incoming one is
unknown (mapc_sync.c: rcpi=CASE WHEN excluded.rcpi>0 AND excluded.rcpi<>255).

So the address of the station being polled is restored before the update.
For a leg it changes nothing - m is already that leg. For the MLD it is the
whole fix, and the borrowed measurement finally lands where it was meant to.

Verify: with an MLO client parked on one link, sta.rcpi for the MLD address
must track that link's rcpi instead of holding a constant.

Signed-off-by: Petr Wozniak <petr.wozniak@gmail.com>
---
--- a/src/agent.c
+++ b/src/agent.c
@@ -1582,6 +1582,33 @@
 			 */
 			sta = *m;
 
+			/* The station we were asked about keeps its address.
+			 *
+			 * 99999996 stopped the fallback from borrowing another
+			 * link's identity, but left one case: an MLD-level
+			 * station has no link of its own. No entry in mlo_links
+			 * carries the MLD address, so the search above falls
+			 * through to sta[0] and the copy takes link 0's address -
+			 * and update_sta_entry() then looks THAT up instead of
+			 * the MLD, exactly the failure 99999996 was written to
+			 * remove.
+			 *
+			 * Measured 2026-09-07 on the bedroom node. The Associated
+			 * STA Link Metrics TLV is generated from the MLD-level
+			 * struct sta, and its rcpi sat at 152 (-34 dBm) through
+			 * six samples a minute apart while the only measured leg
+			 * moved between -52 and -50 dBm. The per-leg rows were
+			 * correct at the same time (rcpi 114 = -53 dBm), so the
+			 * defect is the MLD entry alone: nothing was refreshing
+			 * it, and the controller's upsert keeps the previous
+			 * value when the new one is unknown, so a number measured
+			 * once at association survived as if it were current.
+			 *
+			 * For a leg this is a no-op - m is already that leg. For
+			 * the MLD it is the whole fix.
+			 */
+			memcpy(sta.macaddr, s->macaddr, 6);
+
 			if (sta.rssi_avg == 0) {
 				struct wifi_sta *best = NULL;
 
